1
00:00:00,000 --> 00:00:03,641
Sweetheart, last Christmas my grandson spent the

2
00:00:03,641 --> 00:00:05,842
whole afternoon on his phone,

3
00:00:05,842 --> 00:00:07,283
his sister on hers,

4
00:00:07,283 --> 00:00:10,242
and their parents on the couch watching

5
00:00:10,242 --> 00:00:10,774
a game.

6
00:00:10,954 --> 00:00:12,265
Nobody talked.

7
00:00:12,445 --> 00:00:13,757
Nobody sang.

8
00:00:13,937 --> 00:00:15,793
And after everybody left,

9
00:00:15,793 --> 00:00:17,648
I sat in my kitchen and I

10
00:00:17,648 --> 00:00:20,246
thought, when did we stop doing all

11
00:00:20,246 --> 00:00:23,215
the things that made Christmas feel like

12
00:00:23,215 --> 00:00:23,957
Christmas?

13
00:00:24,137 --> 00:00:26,424
Because it wasn't the presents.

14
00:00:26,604 --> 00:00:28,334
It was never the presents.

15
00:00:28,683 --> 00:00:31,286
So tonight I'm giving you ten Christmas

16
00:00:31,286 --> 00:00:33,954
traditions that almost everybody forgot.

17
00:00:34,134 --> 00:00:36,844
The ones my mother and Grandma Pearl

18
00:00:36,844 --> 00:00:38,500
did every single year.

19
00:00:38,680 --> 00:00:40,444
They cost almost nothing,

20
00:00:40,444 --> 00:00:42,773
and every one of them still works

21
00:00:42,773 --> 00:00:43,196
today.

22
00:00:43,376 --> 00:00:45,665
Pick two or three for this year,

23
00:00:45,665 --> 00:00:48,311
honey, and watch what happens to your

24
00:00:48,311 --> 00:00:48,740
house.

25
00:00:48,920 --> 00:00:50,255
And stay with me,

26
00:00:50,255 --> 00:00:53,160
because the last one is the tradition

27
00:00:53,160 --> 00:00:54,887
I hold closest of all.

28
00:00:55,238 --> 00:00:56,747
Tradition number one.

29
00:00:56,927 --> 00:00:58,715
The Advent candle.

30
00:00:58,895 --> 00:01:01,120
On the first Sunday of December,

31
00:01:01,120 --> 00:01:03,276
my mother set four candles in a

32
00:01:03,276 --> 00:01:05,780
ring of pine branches on the kitchen

33
00:01:05,780 --> 00:01:06,197
table.

34
00:01:06,377 --> 00:01:09,044
Every Sunday night after supper,

35
00:01:09,044 --> 00:01:10,378
we lit one more,

36
00:01:10,378 --> 00:01:12,962
my father read a few lines from

37
00:01:12,962 --> 00:01:15,712
the Bible, and we sang one carol.

38
00:01:15,892 --> 00:01:18,047
Four Sundays, four candles,

39
00:01:18,047 --> 00:01:20,282
and by the last one we could

40
00:01:20,282 --> 00:01:21,639
hardly sit still.

41
00:01:21,989 --> 00:01:23,463
How to do it today.

42
00:01:23,643 --> 00:01:26,539
Four plain candles from the dollar store,

43
00:01:26,539 --> 00:01:28,164
branches from the yard,

44
00:01:28,164 --> 00:01:30,989
and fifteen minutes on Sunday night with

45
00:01:30,989 --> 00:01:32,896
the phones in another room.

46
00:01:33,076 --> 00:01:36,236
Read something, sing one song,

47
00:01:36,236 --> 00:01:37,395
even badly.

48
00:01:37,575 --> 00:01:40,723
My grandchildren rolled their eyes the first

49
00:01:40,723 --> 00:01:41,081
year.

50
00:01:41,261 --> 00:01:43,630
Now they're the ones who remind me.

51
00:01:43,980 --> 00:01:45,570
Tradition number two.

52
00:01:45,750 --> 00:01:46,667
Caroling.

53
00:01:46,847 --> 00:01:49,091
The Saturday before Christmas,

54
00:01:49,091 --> 00:01:51,933
our church choir walked the streets of

55
00:01:51,933 --> 00:01:53,579
Mansfield in the snow,

56
00:01:53,579 --> 00:01:56,047
stopping at the houses of the old

57
00:01:56,047 --> 00:01:57,842
people and the shut-ins.

58
00:01:58,022 --> 00:02:00,676
Danny and I went with our mittens

59
00:02:00,676 --> 00:02:02,527
and a thermos of cocoa.

60
00:02:02,707 --> 00:02:04,030
Old Mr.

61
00:02:04,210 --> 00:02:07,115
Kessler cried every year when we sang

62
00:02:07,115 --> 00:02:09,156
Silent Night on his porch.

63
00:02:09,336 --> 00:02:11,710
He lived alone after the war took

64
00:02:11,710 --> 00:02:12,285
his son.

65
00:02:12,635 --> 00:02:15,386
Today, honey, you don't need a choir.

66
00:02:15,566 --> 00:02:17,909
Take the children to the house of

67
00:02:17,909 --> 00:02:19,968
one neighbor who lives alone,

68
00:02:19,968 --> 00:02:20,961
ring the bell,

69
00:02:20,961 --> 00:02:22,381
and sing two carols.

70
00:02:22,561 --> 00:02:24,500
Bring a plate of cookies.

71
00:02:24,680 --> 00:02:25,597
That's it.

72
00:02:25,777 --> 00:02:26,773
I promise you,

73
00:02:26,773 --> 00:02:28,905
it will be the only visit they

74
00:02:28,905 --> 00:02:30,967
remember that whole December.

75
00:02:31,317 --> 00:02:33,104
Tradition number three.

76
00:02:33,284 --> 00:02:35,328
Writing Christmas letters.

77
00:02:35,508 --> 00:02:37,408
Real ones, on paper,

78
00:02:37,408 --> 00:02:38,643
with a stamp.

79
00:02:38,823 --> 00:02:41,392
My mother sat at the kitchen table

80
00:02:41,392 --> 00:02:44,718
every evening in December writing to cousins

81
00:02:44,718 --> 00:02:47,741
in Pennsylvania, her old school friends,

82
00:02:47,741 --> 00:02:49,933
and my father's army buddies.

83
00:02:50,113 --> 00:02:52,426
Each letter was a whole page,

84
00:02:52,426 --> 00:02:54,421
in her round handwriting.

85
00:02:54,601 --> 00:02:56,622
And when the replies came,

86
00:02:56,622 --> 00:02:59,187
she read them out loud at supper.

87
00:02:59,537 --> 00:03:00,830
Do it this year,

88
00:03:00,830 --> 00:03:01,719
sweetheart.

89
00:03:01,899 --> 00:03:04,663
Pick five people who matter and write

90
00:03:04,663 --> 00:03:06,381
each one a real letter.

91
00:03:06,561 --> 00:03:09,150
Not a printed card with a photo.

92
00:03:09,330 --> 00:03:11,153
A page in your own hand,

93
00:03:11,153 --> 00:03:14,267
telling them one thing you remember about

94
00:03:14,267 --> 00:03:14,647
them.

95
00:03:14,827 --> 00:03:16,942
I write mine on the first week

96
00:03:16,942 --> 00:03:17,788
of December.

97
00:03:17,968 --> 00:03:20,290
Some of those people are gone now,

98
00:03:20,290 --> 00:03:22,749
and their families have told me they

99
00:03:22,749 --> 00:03:23,842
kept my letters.

100
00:03:24,022 --> 00:03:25,929
In a minute I'll tell you the

101
00:03:25,929 --> 00:03:28,492
one my grandchildren won't let me skip.

102
00:03:28,842 --> 00:03:30,525
Tradition number four.

103
00:03:30,705 --> 00:03:33,294
Reading aloud on Christmas Eve.

104
00:03:33,474 --> 00:03:36,251
Every year, my father read The Night

105
00:03:36,251 --> 00:03:38,874
Before Christmas to us in front of

106
00:03:38,874 --> 00:03:41,343
the tree, in his big deep voice,

107
00:03:41,343 --> 00:03:42,963
and Luke chapter two,

108
00:03:42,963 --> 00:03:45,200
the shepherds and the angels.

109
00:03:45,380 --> 00:03:46,878
He did the voices.

110
00:03:47,058 --> 00:03:48,822
He did Santa's laugh.

111
00:03:49,002 --> 00:03:50,307
We knew every word,

112
00:03:50,307 --> 00:03:52,503
and we'd say them along with him

113
00:03:52,503 --> 00:03:53,670
under our breath.

114
00:03:54,020 --> 00:03:56,788
Today, honey, turn off the television at

115
00:03:56,788 --> 00:03:58,933
seven o'clock on Christmas Eve,

116
00:03:58,933 --> 00:04:01,217
and have the oldest person in the

117
00:04:01,217 --> 00:04:02,878
house read to everybody.

118
00:04:03,058 --> 00:04:05,497
Use the same book every year,

119
00:04:05,497 --> 00:04:07,179
so it becomes yours.

120
00:04:07,359 --> 00:04:09,711
The year after my father died,

121
00:04:09,711 --> 00:04:11,828
Danny read it in his place,

122
00:04:11,828 --> 00:04:14,415
and halfway through we all had to

123
00:04:14,415 --> 00:04:17,472
stop because nobody could see the page.

124
00:04:17,822 --> 00:04:19,586
Tradition number five.

125
00:04:19,766 --> 00:04:21,461
The extra stocking.

126
00:04:21,641 --> 00:04:24,320
My mother hung one more stocking than

127
00:04:24,320 --> 00:04:25,333
we had people.

128
00:04:25,513 --> 00:04:28,187
All December, every time we went to

129
00:04:28,187 --> 00:04:30,784
the store, we put something in it.

130
00:04:30,964 --> 00:04:32,369
A pair of socks.

131
00:04:32,549 --> 00:04:33,989
A bar of soap.

132
00:04:34,169 --> 00:04:35,295
An orange.

133
00:04:35,475 --> 00:04:36,520
A pencil.

134
00:04:36,700 --> 00:04:38,649
On Christmas Eve we took it to

135
00:04:38,649 --> 00:04:39,884
the Salvation Army,

136
00:04:39,884 --> 00:04:41,704
for a child we'd never meet.

137
00:04:42,054 --> 00:04:43,783
This one I want you to do

138
00:04:43,783 --> 00:04:45,304
this year, sweetheart.

139
00:04:45,484 --> 00:04:48,607
It teaches children something no present can.

140
00:04:48,787 --> 00:04:50,996
Let them choose what goes in,

141
00:04:50,996 --> 00:04:53,128
and let them carry it to the

142
00:04:53,128 --> 00:04:54,650
drop-off themselves.

143
00:04:54,830 --> 00:04:58,384
Every church, every shelter in America will

144
00:04:58,384 --> 00:04:59,045
take it.

145
00:04:59,225 --> 00:05:01,851
My grandson filled one at six years

146
00:05:01,851 --> 00:05:03,953
old, and he asked me why the

147
00:05:03,953 --> 00:05:06,354
other boy didn't have a daddy to

148
00:05:06,354 --> 00:05:07,480
buy him things.

149
00:05:07,660 --> 00:05:10,335
That was the best Christmas lesson I

150
00:05:10,335 --> 00:05:11,003
ever saw.

151
00:05:11,183 --> 00:05:12,870
If this is helping you,

152
00:05:12,870 --> 00:05:15,363
honey, tap the like button for me.

153
00:05:15,543 --> 00:05:18,109
It helps Grandma more than you know.

154
00:05:18,459 --> 00:05:20,270
Tradition number six.

155
00:05:20,450 --> 00:05:21,947
The Christmas Eve walk.

156
00:05:22,127 --> 00:05:24,685
After supper, my father put on his

157
00:05:24,685 --> 00:05:25,663
hat and said,

158
00:05:25,663 --> 00:05:27,770
let's go look at the lights.

159
00:05:27,950 --> 00:05:30,236
And the four of us walked around

160
00:05:30,236 --> 00:05:32,309
the neighborhood in the cold,

161
00:05:32,309 --> 00:05:34,023
looking at every window,

162
00:05:34,023 --> 00:05:35,810
every candle, every tree.

163
00:05:35,990 --> 00:05:37,885
Then we came home to cocoa,

164
00:05:37,885 --> 00:05:39,990
and that was the only night we

165
00:05:39,990 --> 00:05:42,306
were allowed to stay up past ten.

166
00:05:42,656 --> 00:05:45,061
Bundle up the whole family and do

167
00:05:45,061 --> 00:05:45,279
it.

168
00:05:45,459 --> 00:05:47,538
Thirty minutes, no phones,

169
00:05:47,538 --> 00:05:50,497
just walking and pointing and saying,

170
00:05:50,497 --> 00:05:51,857
look at that one.

171
00:05:52,037 --> 00:05:53,224
If it's too cold,

172
00:05:53,224 --> 00:05:55,459
pile everybody in the car with a

173
00:05:55,459 --> 00:05:58,323
thermos of cocoa and drive slowly through

174
00:05:58,323 --> 00:06:00,419
the best neighborhood in town.

175
00:06:00,599 --> 00:06:03,365
My grandchildren call it the light tour,

176
00:06:03,365 --> 00:06:05,440
and they won't let me skip it.

177
00:06:05,791 --> 00:06:07,555
Tradition number seven.

178
00:06:07,735 --> 00:06:09,660
An orange in the toe of the

179
00:06:09,660 --> 00:06:10,301
stocking.

180
00:06:10,481 --> 00:06:12,324
In the nineteen thirties,

181
00:06:12,324 --> 00:06:14,903
an orange in December was a luxury,

182
00:06:14,903 --> 00:06:17,925
and Grandma Pearl got one every Christmas

183
00:06:17,925 --> 00:06:20,431
of her childhood and nothing else.

184
00:06:20,611 --> 00:06:22,542
So she put an orange in the

185
00:06:22,542 --> 00:06:24,974
toe of every stocking for the rest

186
00:06:24,974 --> 00:06:25,832
of her life,

187
00:06:25,832 --> 00:06:27,334
and my mother did it,

188
00:06:27,334 --> 00:06:28,622
and I still do it.

189
00:06:28,802 --> 00:06:31,262
It's how we remember that we didn't

190
00:06:31,262 --> 00:06:32,668
always have so much.

191
00:06:33,018 --> 00:06:35,808
Put one in every stocking this year,

192
00:06:35,808 --> 00:06:38,056
honey, right down in the toe.

193
00:06:38,236 --> 00:06:40,410
And when the children ask why,

194
00:06:40,410 --> 00:06:42,875
tell them about the little girl in

195
00:06:42,875 --> 00:06:46,063
nineteen thirty-one who woke up on Christmas

196
00:06:46,063 --> 00:06:49,107
morning, found one orange in her stocking,

197
00:06:49,107 --> 00:06:51,716
and thought she was the richest girl

198
00:06:51,716 --> 00:06:52,296
in Ohio.

199
00:06:52,476 --> 00:06:55,111
That little girl was my grandmother.

200
00:06:55,291 --> 00:06:57,248
Every orange is a story,

201
00:06:57,248 --> 00:06:58,960
if somebody tells it.

202
00:06:59,140 --> 00:07:01,855
But first, one that will change how

203
00:07:01,855 --> 00:07:04,492
your whole house feels in January.

204
00:07:04,843 --> 00:07:06,201
Tradition number eight.

205
00:07:06,381 --> 00:07:09,690
Keeping Christmas until the sixth of January.

206
00:07:09,870 --> 00:07:12,115
Today the tree goes out on the

207
00:07:12,115 --> 00:07:14,885
twenty-sixth, like the party is over.

208
00:07:15,065 --> 00:07:17,145
But Grandma Pearl kept the tree,

209
00:07:17,145 --> 00:07:19,421
the candles and the music going for

210
00:07:19,421 --> 00:07:21,306
the twelve days of Christmas,

211
00:07:21,306 --> 00:07:23,061
and on the sixth we had one

212
00:07:23,061 --> 00:07:25,271
more little party with the last of

213
00:07:25,271 --> 00:07:28,196
the cookies before the decorations came down.

214
00:07:28,376 --> 00:07:30,443
Christmas wasn't one day.

215
00:07:30,623 --> 00:07:32,016
It was twelve.

216
00:07:32,366 --> 00:07:33,724
Try it this year.

217
00:07:33,904 --> 00:07:36,403
Leave the tree up until the sixth

218
00:07:36,403 --> 00:07:37,236
of January.

219
00:07:37,416 --> 00:07:39,841
Keep one candle lit at supper every

220
00:07:39,841 --> 00:07:41,572
night of the twelve days.

221
00:07:41,752 --> 00:07:42,997
And on the sixth,

222
00:07:42,997 --> 00:07:44,461
have a little party,

223
00:07:44,461 --> 00:07:45,632
just the family,

224
00:07:45,632 --> 00:07:47,754
with the last of the cookies,

225
00:07:47,754 --> 00:07:50,609
and let the children take the ornaments

226
00:07:50,609 --> 00:07:52,805
down and wrap them one by one.

227
00:07:52,985 --> 00:07:55,023
It turns the saddest day of the

228
00:07:55,023 --> 00:07:57,060
year into one more celebration.

229
00:07:57,411 --> 00:07:59,059
Tradition number nine.

230
00:07:59,239 --> 00:08:01,689
Thank-you notes before New Year's.

231
00:08:01,869 --> 00:08:03,776
My mother sat us down on the

232
00:08:03,776 --> 00:08:06,637
twenty-seventh with a stack of note cards,

233
00:08:06,637 --> 00:08:08,953
and we couldn't play with a single

234
00:08:08,953 --> 00:08:11,337
new toy until we'd written to every

235
00:08:11,337 --> 00:08:13,653
aunt and uncle who sent something.

236
00:08:13,833 --> 00:08:15,331
Three sentences.

237
00:08:15,511 --> 00:08:17,811
Thank you, what I'll do with it,

238
00:08:17,811 --> 00:08:19,249
and that I love you.

239
00:08:19,429 --> 00:08:21,124
I hated it then.

240
00:08:21,304 --> 00:08:23,486
Now I know it's why my aunts

241
00:08:23,486 --> 00:08:24,810
loved us so much.

242
00:08:24,990 --> 00:08:26,200
And coming up,

243
00:08:26,200 --> 00:08:27,323
the last one,

244
00:08:27,323 --> 00:08:29,309
the one I hold closest.

245
00:08:29,659 --> 00:08:32,516
Give your children plain note cards and

246
00:08:32,516 --> 00:08:33,321
a good pen,

247
00:08:33,321 --> 00:08:35,738
honey, and sit down with them and

248
00:08:35,738 --> 00:08:38,082
write your own notes at the same

249
00:08:38,082 --> 00:08:38,448
time.

250
00:08:38,628 --> 00:08:39,696
They'll grumble.

251
00:08:39,876 --> 00:08:41,026
Let them.

252
00:08:41,206 --> 00:08:43,306
One day an aunt will tell your

253
00:08:43,306 --> 00:08:45,895
daughter she kept every note she ever

254
00:08:45,895 --> 00:08:47,155
sent, in a drawer,

255
00:08:47,155 --> 00:08:48,485
tied with a ribbon.

256
00:08:48,665 --> 00:08:49,720
My Aunt Ruth,

257
00:08:49,720 --> 00:08:51,668
the one I was named for,

258
00:08:51,668 --> 00:08:53,048
did exactly that,

259
00:08:53,048 --> 00:08:55,808
and her daughter gave them back to

260
00:08:55,808 --> 00:08:57,512
me after the funeral.

261
00:08:57,861 --> 00:08:59,796
And tradition number ten,

262
00:08:59,796 --> 00:09:01,577
the one I hold closest.

263
00:09:01,757 --> 00:09:03,220
The empty chair.

264
00:09:03,400 --> 00:09:06,237
When somebody in the family was gone,

265
00:09:06,237 --> 00:09:07,158
away at war,

266
00:09:07,158 --> 00:09:08,922
or passed on that year,

267
00:09:08,922 --> 00:09:11,376
my mother set their place at the

268
00:09:11,376 --> 00:09:13,140
Christmas table anyway.

269
00:09:13,320 --> 00:09:15,066
A plate, a glass,

270
00:09:15,066 --> 00:09:15,991
a candle.

271
00:09:16,171 --> 00:09:17,532
And before grace,

272
00:09:17,532 --> 00:09:19,773
each of us said one thing we

273
00:09:19,773 --> 00:09:21,534
remembered about them.

274
00:09:21,714 --> 00:09:23,504
In nineteen sixty-eight,

275
00:09:23,504 --> 00:09:25,592
the empty chair was Danny's,

276
00:09:25,592 --> 00:09:27,828
and that year we said a prayer

277
00:09:27,828 --> 00:09:28,425
instead.

278
00:09:28,775 --> 00:09:31,158
Do it this year if you've lost

279
00:09:31,158 --> 00:09:32,350
someone, honey.

280
00:09:32,530 --> 00:09:34,968
It doesn't make Christmas sadder.

281
00:09:35,148 --> 00:09:36,716
It makes it fuller.

282
00:09:36,896 --> 00:09:39,590
The children learn who came before them,

283
00:09:39,590 --> 00:09:41,678
and the person you miss gets to

284
00:09:41,678 --> 00:09:43,699
be at the table one more time.

285
00:09:44,050 --> 00:09:46,679
And here's one more small tradition to

286
00:09:46,679 --> 00:09:47,440
go with it.

287
00:09:47,620 --> 00:09:49,350
Keep a Christmas book.

288
00:09:49,530 --> 00:09:52,406
A plain notebook that comes out every

289
00:09:52,406 --> 00:09:53,105
December.

290
00:09:53,285 --> 00:09:55,750
Every year, write down who was at

291
00:09:55,750 --> 00:09:57,543
the table, what you ate,

292
00:09:57,543 --> 00:09:59,186
what the children got,

293
00:09:59,186 --> 00:10:01,726
and one funny thing somebody said.

294
00:10:01,906 --> 00:10:05,284
My mother started ours in nineteen forty-six.

295
00:10:05,464 --> 00:10:06,975
I have it on my shelf,

296
00:10:06,975 --> 00:10:09,240
and reading it is like having all

297
00:10:09,240 --> 00:10:11,026
of them in the room again.

298
00:10:11,376 --> 00:10:12,409
So there they are.

299
00:10:12,589 --> 00:10:14,272
The Advent candles.

300
00:10:14,452 --> 00:10:16,472
Caroling for one neighbor.

301
00:10:16,652 --> 00:10:18,127
Real letters.

302
00:10:18,307 --> 00:10:20,629
Reading aloud on Christmas Eve.

303
00:10:20,809 --> 00:10:22,957
A stocking for a stranger.

304
00:10:23,137 --> 00:10:25,006
The walk to see the lights.

305
00:10:25,186 --> 00:10:26,939
An orange in the toe.

306
00:10:27,119 --> 00:10:29,278
Twelve days, not one.

307
00:10:29,458 --> 00:10:30,770
Thank-you notes.

308
00:10:30,950 --> 00:10:32,483
And the empty chair.

309
00:10:32,663 --> 00:10:34,798
None of them cost more than a

310
00:10:34,798 --> 00:10:35,681
few dollars.

311
00:10:35,861 --> 00:10:37,746
All of them are worth more than

312
00:10:37,746 --> 00:10:39,205
anything under the tree.

313
00:10:39,556 --> 00:10:42,064
Now tell me in the comments,

314
00:10:42,064 --> 00:10:43,050
sweetheart.

315
00:10:43,230 --> 00:10:46,055
Which tradition did your family have that

316
00:10:46,055 --> 00:10:47,433
nobody does anymore?

317
00:10:47,613 --> 00:10:48,780
I read every one,

318
00:10:48,780 --> 00:10:50,840
and some of yours might end up

319
00:10:50,840 --> 00:10:51,595
in a video.

320
00:10:51,775 --> 00:10:54,363
And if you want another one tonight,

321
00:10:54,363 --> 00:10:57,670
the whole playlist of Grandma's Christmas tips

322
00:10:57,670 --> 00:10:59,826
is right there on your screen,

323
00:10:59,826 --> 00:11:01,839
and I'll see you in the next

324
00:11:01,839 --> 00:11:02,271
video.

325
00:11:02,451 --> 00:11:04,088
Good night, honey.

326
00:11:04,268 --> 00:11:05,765
Merry Christmas.
